<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements of the Python interpreters that run module code on macOS, and of nothing else: the node runtime's
  bin/python3.12 (scripts/package-macos.sh, /Library/Oarbank/bin/runtime) and the coordinator build's
  python/bin/python3.12 (scripts/build-coordinator.sh, scripts/package-coordinator-macos.sh). Module environments
  (uv venv) link to these interpreters, so their processes carry the same signature. scripts/check-macos-signing.py
  refuses a build whose interpreter lacks this set or carries anything more; docs/release-signing.md, "macOS code
  signatures", says why.

  com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
    With a Developer ID, the interpreters are signed with the hardened runtime (needed for notarization), and the
    hardened runtime turns on library validation: the process may then map only code signed by Apple or by the same
    Team ID as its executable. Modules install third-party wheels from PyPI (numpy, pysam, ...) whose extension
    modules and bundled libraries are signed ad hoc or by their own teams, so dlopen refuses them ("mapping process
    and mapped file (non-platform) have different Team IDs"). This entitlement lets the interpreter load them. Code
    must still carry a valid signature (every arm64 binary does; the linker signs ad hoc), and the hardened runtime's
    other protections stay on.
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/hardened-runtime
    Notarization accepts it: it is one of the hardened runtime's documented exceptions, not a debugging entitlement
    such as com.apple.security.get-task-allow, which the notary service refuses.
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/resolving-common-notarization-issues

  Deliberately absent:
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables: nothing in Oarbank sets DYLD_* for these processes, and
      the runtime is relocatable without them (@rpath and @loader_path).
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory, com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit: CPython 3.12 does not
      write executable memory (ctypes callbacks use libffi's static trampolines, which the build's canary calls under
      the signed interpreter). A wheel that needs a JIT would be a separate decision, not part of this set.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
